  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a pronoun?",
      "a": "A pronoun is a word that takes the place of a noun so we don't repeat it, like he, she, it, they, me and us.",
      "asks": [
        "What does pronoun mean?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Is it me or I?",
      "a": "Take the other person out and see what sounds right. Priya and I went, because I went. Mum called Priya and me, because Mum called me.",
      "asks": [
        "Sam and I or Sam and me?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What are possessive pronouns?",
      "a": "They show who owns something: mine, yours, his, hers, ours and theirs. They never take an apostrophe.",
      "asks": [
        "Does hers have an apostrophe?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Is it a pronoun?",
      "a": "Yes, it stands in for a thing or an animal: The kite is red. It flies high.",
      "asks": [
        "What does it stand for?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Can they mean one person?",
      "a": "Yes. They is often used for one person when we do not know or do not need to say if they are a he or a she: Someone left their umbrella.",
      "asks": [
        "Singular they"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Singular they, for one person, has been used in English for over 600 years, including by Chaucer.",
    "Japanese often leaves pronouns out altogether when it is clear who is meant."
  ],
